= Discussion <chapter:discussion>

This chapter interprets the results in relation to the research question. It first examines how pretraining affects online learning and final physical control, including normal-operation chlorine excess alongside arsenic burden, clearance timing, and chemical use. The discussion then weighs these outcomes against the simulator interactions and computational cost of preparation, and considers opportunities for reuse and parallel execution. Finally, it assesses the supporting forecasting evidence and the limitations that bound the interpretation and practical relevance of the findings.

== Answer to the Research Question

The results support an early online learning advantage from #acr("MPC")-guided pretraining in the evaluated Hanoi scenarios under the common online interaction budget. The pretrained approach achieves a higher mean validation return at every checkpoint in @fig:validation-return, including before any #acr("PPO") update. The advantage becomes much smaller as the baseline approach improves, but remains positive in four of the five matched seed pairs at the budget endpoint. For the online sample-efficiency part of the research question, pretraining therefore supplies useful initial behavior and maintains better mean performance throughout the evaluated training range.

The shortfall difference remains uncertain. For final physical control, the selected pretrained policies have lower transported #acr("As(III)") burden and greater mean clearance lead on the paired eligible events than the selected baseline policies, alongside higher normal-operation chlorine excess and chlorine use. No event-time chlorine ceiling violations occur in the applicable test observations. These four paired effects have confidence intervals excluding zero in @tab:final-control-metrics, and their directions agree across all five seeds in @tab:seed-differences in @section:appendix:numerical-results. The exploratory decomposition of the chlorine excess metric locates nearly the entire excess difference in the first #hour[12] after reference clearance. Pretraining thus improves two complementary aspects of contamination treatment under the modeled reaction, while the additional chlorine exceedances occur primarily during the subsequent return to normal operation. Its benefit depends on the balance between treatment, residual concentrations, and chemical use required by the intended control application.


== Online Learning and Final Control

The zero-interaction comparison isolates what the initialization provides before reward-based fine-tuning. At this point, the behavior-cloned policy already controls the network more effectively under the validation objective than the ordinarily initialized policy. Because the forecaster and optimizer are absent during policy evaluation, this result shows that the recurrent actor can use the transferred initialization in closed-loop control. It does not establish which transferred component produces the improvement.

The initial mean difference should nevertheless be interpreted alongside the individual seed trajectories. One baseline seed begins with a particularly large negative return, which strongly influences the mean and its spread in @fig:validation-return. The advantage is therefore less uniform in magnitude than the mean curve alone suggests. Its continuation beyond the first few checkpoints matters more than the extreme initial gap, because it shows that the comparison is not determined only by one poor ordinary initialization.

The initial cloned policy also provides a concrete reference for the amount of learning required by the baseline approach. Its mean validation return is approximately #return-mean(initial-learning.at("pretrained-mean"), initial-learning.at("pretrained-sd")). The baseline approach first exceeds this value at #num(actual-interactions(initial-mean-crossing.nominal)) actual interactions, corresponding to the nominal #num(initial-mean-crossing.nominal)-interaction checkpoint. Within individual seed pairs, the first observed crossing of the respective pretrained zero-interaction return occurs between #num[100000] and #num[850000] nominal interactions. These are retrospective comparisons with the cloned initialization, rather than interaction savings against a predefined control target. Checkpoint spacing limits their temporal resolution, and equality of return would not imply equality of the six physical metrics. Nevertheless, they show that the offline initialization supplies behavior that the baseline approach otherwise acquires only after substantial online learning.

Fine-tuning remains useful after cloning. The pretrained approach's mean return improves substantially from its initial value and reaches a much better range during #acr("PPO"). Meanwhile, the baseline approach continues to improve, especially late in training, narrowing the mean difference to approximately #return-num(final-learning.at("difference-mean")) at the endpoint. This pattern supports an initialization advantage within the investigated budget. The remaining gap does not establish different asymptotic performance.

The endpoint return and the final physical comparison answer different questions. The former describes both approaches at the same cumulative interaction count. The latter evaluates the checkpoints selected by their highest mean validation return, following @section:evaluation:policy-selection. As @tab:validation-selected-checkpoints in @section:appendix:numerical-results shows, these checkpoints can occur at different stages of training. Earlier selection of several pretrained checkpoints suggests that useful policies become available sooner, but all runs completed the common budget. Training after the selected checkpoint therefore remains part of the measured cost.

The additional comparisons in @section:results:additional-variants qualify the return advantage at the common online interaction budget. At #num(extended-baseline-first-crossing) nominal interactions, the extended-budget baseline first reaches the pretrained approach's mean validation return at the one-million-interaction endpoint. It ends slightly above this reference after four million nominal interactions. Its selected policies have a lower mean validation return than the selected pretrained policies, although the paired confidence interval for this difference spans zero. The selected pretrained checkpoints occur earlier in online training. Additional baseline training therefore narrows the difference in validation return, but the comparison establishes neither a return advantage after checkpoint selection nor equivalence.

The variant without actor weights has higher mean return than the ordinary baseline during most of training, but this advantage is absent at the endpoint. Full pretraining has higher validation return both at the endpoint and after checkpoint selection, with differences in the same direction across all five seed pairs and confidence intervals excluding zero. This supports a contribution from the cloned actor weights beyond the learning advantage provided by prepared normalization and initial action standard deviation. However, their contribution without these two transferred components remains untested.

Finally, return summarizes the particular reward objective defined in @section:methodology:reward-function. It combines chlorine tracking, concentration movement, and action changes, with relaxed upper-concentration weighting during recovery. It does not directly minimize transported arsenic burden or injected chlorine mass. The physical test metrics are consequently needed to determine what the return advantage means for treatment and dosing.


== Physical Control Trade-offs <section:discussion:trade-offs>

When assessing the final physical control performance, we give priority to lower transported #acr("As(III)") burden and earlier persistent clearance when weighing these gains against chlorine use and post-clearance chlorine excess.

The final metrics indicate that the selected baseline policies and selected pretrained policies generally maintain the normal-operation lower residual bound in the evaluated cases. The shortfall fractions are small in absolute terms, but the paired confidence interval includes both improvement and deterioration. This uncertainty does not establish equivalence. The selected pretrained policy also has a higher normal-operation excess fraction in every seed pair. Since this difference is measured in percentage points, its absolute magnitude in @tab:final-control-metrics is more informative than a large relative change from a small baseline value.

The exploratory decomposition reported in #chapter-ref(<chapter:results>) locates almost all chlorine excess under both policies in the first #hour[12] after reference clearance and nearly the entire pretrained-minus-baseline difference in these same periods. Other reference-normal periods contribute very little under either policy. The metric defined in @section:evaluation:chlorine-shortfalls-and-excesses therefore includes the return to normal operation after contamination. Its higher value under pretraining does not by itself establish generally less stable or poorer control during normal operation. The exceedances remain departures from the normal chlorine bound and are relevant when assessing the residual concentrations left after treatment.

The clearest treatment benefit is the reduction in transported #acr("As(III)") burden. The mean burden of the selected pretrained policies is about #fixed-pct(100 * burden-reduction, digits: 1) lower than that of the selected baseline policies. The lower burden is consistent across seed summaries, while Panel (a) of @fig:test-case-burden shows lower pretrained mean burden in 78 of the 100 test cases, equal burden in four, and higher burden in 18. The benefit is therefore broad across the fixed test catalog but does not apply to every hydraulic scenario and contamination schedule.

Clearance timing complements the integrated burden by showing whether relevant transported #acr("As(III)") persists for less time. On average, the selected pretrained policies reach persistent clearance #duration-text(clearance-summary.at("difference-mean") * 3600) earlier than the selected baseline policies across the paired eligible events. Panel (b) of @fig:test-case-burden shows that lower burden and greater clearance lead occur together in every matched seed summary. The timing advantage is modest, and its confidence interval extends close to zero.

Neither approach exceeded the event-time chlorine ceiling in the eligible test observations. The treatment advantage under pretraining is therefore not accompanied by additional event-time ceiling violations. Both approaches share a reward that continues to penalize above-target chlorine during recovery, although with substantially reduced weighting. The actuator bounds in @section:methodology:control-action-and-actuator-constraints constrain source injection, but do not impose hard limits on every nodal chlorine concentration.

These treatment gains are accompanied by about #fixed-pct(100 * use-increase, digits: 1) greater chlorine use. The use decomposition places most of the additional chlorine in the main-window reference-event periods, while the excess decomposition places nearly all additional exceedances in the period after reference clearance. Under matched hydraulic conditions and identical reaction parameters, the higher chlorine use, lower transported #acr("As(III)") burden, and earlier clearance suggest a stronger treatment response under the modeled oxidation reaction in @eq:appendix-pipe-reaction-rates in @section:appendix:simulation-configuration. The higher post-clearance excess is consistent with elevated residual chlorine remaining after treatment. However, the comparison does not quantify how much of the additional injected chlorine is consumed by oxidation rather than lost through decay or remaining as residual chlorine. It also does not isolate the contribution of the larger injected mass from that of injection timing and the resulting chlorine availability in the network.

Under the treatment priority adopted above, we favor the selected pretrained policies because their lower transported #acr("As(III)") burden and earlier persistent clearance outweigh the modest increase in chlorine use in the evaluated setting. The absence of additional event-time ceiling violations supports this preference within the observed test cases. The additional normal-operation chlorine excess remains a cost, but its absolute increase is small and occurs almost entirely in the first #hour[12] after reference clearance. With very little additional excess in other reference-normal periods, this pattern supports accepting the higher post-clearance residuals in favor of more effective contamination treatment. Reducing this excess while preserving the treatment gains would improve the balance further.


== Interaction Cost and Computational Cost

The learning advantage uses an additional source of training information. The precomputed offline trajectories across the training, validation, and test partitions require three million simulator steps before online learning. Forecaster training and #acr("MPC") teacher generation reuse these trajectories, so repeated processing does not add to this trajectory-generation cost. Together with one million nominal online interactions, this defines the extended-budget baseline's online interaction budget of four million. Separate simulator checks and control evaluations are excluded from this comparison. The experiment supports better performance per online interaction, but does not establish lower total simulator use.

The stage times in @tab:computational-costs give a similarly qualified result. Mean #acr("PPO") training time is #duration-text(baseline-ppo-cost.mean) per baseline run and #duration-text(pretrained-ppo-cost.mean) per pretrained run, despite their matched interaction counts. This shorter fine-tuning stage does not offset preparation. Across all five seeds, forecasting takes #duration-text(forecaster-cost.total), teacher generation #duration-text(mpc-cost.total), and cloning #duration-text(behavior-cloning-cost.total). Shared trajectory generation adds #duration-text(offline-trajectories-cost.total). Including the separately reported evaluations, the summed stage runtimes are #duration-text(baseline-total-seconds) for the baseline approach and #duration-text(pretrained-total-seconds) for the pretrained approach.

The variant runtimes in @section:results:additional-variants reinforce the distinction between online sample efficiency and total training effort. The extended-budget baseline takes #duration-text(extended-baseline-training-cost.mean) per seed for #acr("PPO") and another #duration-text(extended-baseline-training-cost.validation-mean) for checkpoint validation. Even this longer training remains less costly in measured runtime than fresh pretraining and fine-tuning. Without actor weights, #acr("PPO") takes #duration-text(without-actor-training-cost.mean) per seed. This variant still uses normalization and action standard deviation obtained through the offline pipeline, so its online runtime does not describe an alternative without preparation cost.

Forecasting is the dominant preparation cost. The five-fold design makes each training scenario contribute to fitting four models, while its teacher labels are generated using the remaining model. This exchanges additional model fitting for reuse of one simulator dataset and scenario-level separation between forecasting inputs and teacher queries. It is appropriate when simulator interactions are the scarce resource, but the measured stage times show why minimizing interactions need not minimize preparation time. On the recorded setup, generating the complete offline collection takes less than one hour with eight CPU workers, whereas fitting the 25 forecasters accounts for most of the offline runtime. Fewer folds could reduce this fitting cost while preserving scenario-level separation. However, larger held-out folds would leave fewer training scenarios for each forecaster, which could impair forecast accuracy and the quality of teacher actions. Comparisons should therefore assess whether fewer folds preserve forecast accuracy and final control performance at lower preparation cost.

An alternative would train one forecaster on 80 scenarios, select it using ten validation scenarios, and generate a separate teacher-query collection under new contamination schedules. Cloning could then use the new training demonstrations and their own validation demonstrations. This would require more simulator trajectories but avoid fitting five forecasters per policy seed. With the same hydraulic scenarios, the two datasets differ only in their contamination schedules. It would not preserve the current exclusion of each teacher scenario from forecaster fitting, which was introduced to reduce the risk of overly optimistic MPC action evaluations on scenarios seen during forecaster training. To preserve that stronger condition with one forecaster, the teacher queries would need a separate hydraulic scenario collection. Either design would also require validation data kept out of fitting and a separate final test set.

The measured costs permit an illustrative estimate of the change in preparation time. One fold-specific model takes #duration-text(forecaster-cost.mean) on average and trains on 64 scenarios. If runtime scaled with the number of training windows at unchanged epoch count and throughput, fitting one model on 80 scenarios would take #duration-text(single-forecaster-seconds). Under this assumption, the forecasting training volume would fall by #pct[75] relative to five 64-scenario fits. Keeping teacher-generation and cloning times at their measured means gives #duration-text(single-forecaster-preparation-seconds) per run for these three stages, compared with #duration-text(cross-fitted-preparation-per-seed) in the current pipeline. This estimate excludes new trajectory generation and evaluation. It is a cost projection, since early stopping, model generalization, and the resulting policy quality could change under the alternative design.

Teacher generation would still label the complete training collection. In the current design, each training query is already evaluated using only one fold-specific forecaster, so replacing five folds with one model does not remove four fifths of these queries. A single model would reduce repeated prediction work for validation, where the current teacher uses a five-model ensemble. The resulting total speedup cannot be inferred directly from the number of folds. The estimate above therefore conservatively keeps measured teacher runtime unchanged.

Preparation costs can also be amortized. A published simulation dataset could be reused for further pretraining studies on the same network and compatible demand, reaction, and dosing conditions. Reuse would remove fresh trajectory generation, while changed forecasting models or teacher objectives would still require fitting or relabeling. If an already fitted forecaster and a compatible teacher dataset were reused as well, a new policy would mainly incur cloning and subsequent fine-tuning costs. At the measured means, these two stages together take #duration-text((behavior-cloning-cost.mean + pretrained-ppo-cost.mean)) per run, excluding evaluation. The present experiment deliberately refits the forecasters and regenerates labels for every seed to assess variability in the complete pipeline. Sharing these artifacts across future policies would be a different replication design.

Historical records from a real drinking-water network could also replace simulated offline trajectories if they provide synchronized chlorine measurements, source injections, and the temporal context required by @section:methodology:forecasting-data. This would remove the cost of generating those trajectories in a simulator. Its feasibility depends on whether the records cover the relevant hydraulic conditions, dosing sequences, and disturbances. Routine operation alone may offer insufficient evidence for predicting contamination responses or unfamiliar candidate injections. Operational-data reuse is therefore a possible preparation strategy whose forecast and control validity would need separate evaluation. It does not remove the remaining forecaster fitting, teacher-generation, and cloning stages, or the simulator fine-tuning used in this study.

Parallel execution offers another way to shorten elapsed preparation time. Fold-specific forecasting runs are independent and could be trained simultaneously on separate devices. Teacher labeling can be distributed across scenarios or trajectories because one trajectory's generated actions do not determine another trajectory's query states. Each worker still needs the appropriate trained forecaster, observation history, and reconstructed reward state. Thus, independent task allocation is possible even though the reward calculation within a trajectory is stateful. The measured teacher runs already use eight workers. Assigning more independent trajectory groups to additional machines could shorten elapsed time, but would retain the prediction and optimization work and introduce data-transfer and resource costs.

These alternatives change the practical value of the initialization without changing the evidence from the principal comparison. For a single policy with fresh preparation, the current pipeline is far more expensive than the baseline approach. However, for repeated policy training from reusable artifacts, or under a design that uses one forecaster, the remaining preparation cost could be substantially smaller. In all cases, policies trained with either approach use the same recurrent architecture and do not require forecasting or #acr("MPC") at inference time.


== Meaning of the Supporting Forecast Evaluation

The forecast results assess the predictive basis of the teacher, rather than the quality of its selected actions. Full-horizon mean #acr("MAE") is about #mg-l[0.025] and #acr("RMSE") about #mg-l[0.050] on the ten forecasting test scenarios, as shown in @fig:forecast-full-horizon in @section:appendix:numerical-results. These values are smaller than the #mg-l[0.2] width of the adopted normal-operation band, but remain relevant near its boundaries. An average error below the band width does not ensure correct classification of residual shortfalls or excesses. The higher #acr("RMSE") also makes clear that a single mean absolute error does not describe all prediction deviations.

Both errors increase with the prediction horizon in @fig:forecast-horizon-errors. This suggests greater uncertainty in the concentration predictions used for the teacher's longer-term action comparisons. The measured errors nevertheless suggest that the forecasters approximate the multistep chlorine response reasonably well from the limited inputs defined in @section:methodology:forecasting-data. They use chlorine and injection histories, planned future injections, and calendar features, without direct information about nodal demand or contamination events.

The scope of this evidence also depends on the injection sequences used for evaluation. Both forecaster training and the supporting test use trajectories generated with prescribed injection profiles that expose the models to varied chlorine responses. During teacher generation, however, the optimizer searches for injection sequences with high predicted returns. These sequences may differ from those represented in the offline data. Accuracy on the test trajectories therefore does not establish how accurately the forecasters predict the effects of the candidate sequences used to select teacher actions.

The higher validation return before online training and the subsequent physical test outcomes are therefore stronger evidence for the usefulness of the complete initialization than forecasting errors alone. They show that the pipeline can transfer useful behavior despite imperfect prediction. They do not demonstrate teacher optimality, rule out exploitation of prediction errors, or establish a causal relationship between a fold's forecast error and its policy outcome. Those questions would require evaluating selected candidate sequences in the simulator and relating their prediction and action errors to closed-loop control.


== Limitations

The statistical comparison uses only five matched pairs of training runs. This limits how precisely the average difference between the approaches can be estimated. The confidence intervals describe this uncertainty for the shared offline trajectories and fixed evaluation cases used in this study. They do not capture possible variation from newly generated offline data or different evaluation cases. Within this setting, the confidence intervals for four physical metrics exclude zero, and all five seed pairs agree on the direction of these differences. However, the size of the differences varies across seeds and individual test cases.

The dataset limits how broadly the findings apply. Its hydraulic scenarios differ in demands and uncertain network parameters, but all use the Hanoi topology, the same chlorine source location, and the same actuator constraints. All scenarios also cover the same 25-day period, which captures only a limited part of the seasonal demand patterns provided by LeakDB @vrachimis2018leakdb. The contamination schedules vary, but all follow the same event-generation rules in @section:dataset:arsenic-events. Performance on other networks, in other seasons, or under different contamination settings therefore remains untested.

The physical interpretation depends on the transport, mixing, and reaction assumptions in @section:dataset:simulation-setup. The model tracks free chlorine and #acr("As(III)") and omits longitudinal dispersion. It represents the oxidation of #acr("As(III)") by chlorine without explicitly tracking As(V). A lower transported #acr("As(III)") burden therefore indicates improved treatment under the modeled oxidation reaction. It does not demonstrate a reduction in total arsenic or establish drinking-water safety.

The reported clearance advantage covers only events in which both policies exceed the transported-arsenic threshold, as defined in @section:evaluation:arsenic-clearance-lead. If either policy stays at or below this threshold, the event is excluded from the clearance comparison. The result therefore does not describe all contamination events, whereas integrated arsenic burden covers all test cases. Clearance times are determined in half-hour steps within the fixed observation period, which limits the timing resolution and the duration of follow-up.

The learning curves and checkpoint selection use the same fixed control-validation cases, as defined in @section:evaluation:policy-selection. The selected validation return therefore does not provide an independent assessment of performance on new cases. Separate test cases provide the final physical comparison. Before fine-tuning, the cloned initialization already achieves a higher mean return on the validation cases. However, a test comparison using the six physical metrics is not reported for this initial policy. The results therefore do not show whether the cloned initialization already achieves the treatment and dosing balance observed after fine-tuning.

The principal comparison evaluates the complete initialization, so its effects cannot be attributed to actor weights alone. Actor weights, observation normalization, and the initial action standard deviation change together, while the critic remains ordinarily initialized. The variant without actor weights in @section:results:additional-variants assesses the contribution of actor weights to validation return. Prepared normalization and action standard deviation are retained in this comparison. The individual contributions of these two components and their interactions therefore remain unclear. The additional variants were compared using validation return, without assessing their physical treatment and dosing outcomes. It also remains unclear whether cross-fitting and the choice of folds, the chosen forecasting architecture, or the particular teacher configuration are necessary for the observed advantages in online learning and final contamination treatment.

The teacher and cloning stages do not directly optimize the cloned policy's performance over complete control episodes. The #acr("MPC") teacher evaluates actions over eight hours and uses no terminal value to account for later consequences. Cloning minimizes action #acr("MSE") at sampled query states, without giving extra weight to errors that have greater effects on later control. These query states come from prescribed injection trajectories. The teacher's action labels do not change how those trajectories continue. During closed-loop control, the actor's own actions shape the later states, so it can encounter a different mix of states from those used for cloning. This risk of sequential distribution shift is a known limitation of offline imitation and motivates procedures such as #acr("DAgger") @ross2011dagger. Here, #acr("PPO") allows adaptation through reward feedback. However, the comparison does not show how much of the improvement comes from correcting imitation errors, covering more states, or changing the inherited control strategy.

The findings apply to the observation setting in @tab:methodology-policy-observation, where the controller receives chlorine measurements at all 31 demand nodes. Its performance with fewer sensors, measurement noise, missing values, or sensor delays was not tested. The results therefore do not establish how well the controller would perform under different sensor coverage or measurement conditions.


== Overall Interpretation

The experiment supports the use of offline predictive control to initialize a recurrent policy for the examined partially observable chlorination task. It extends the model-based initialization and model-free fine-tuning approach demonstrated in other control domains @nagabandi2018dynamics through a fixed cross-fitted teacher dataset. The learning and physical test results jointly support useful initialization, with higher mean validation return during online learning and more effective contamination treatment under the adopted priority. These gains are accompanied by modest additional chlorine use and post-clearance excess, while fresh preparation takes more time than is saved during fine-tuning. The practical value of pretraining therefore depends on the relative priority given to treatment and dosing, and on whether preparation can be reused or reduced without losing control quality.
